.TH "easel alicol" 1 "@EASEL_DATE@" "Easel @EASEL_VERSION@" "Easel Manual"

.SH NAME

easel alicol \- select subset of columns from a multiple alignment 

.SH SYNOPSIS

.B easel alicol
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I msafile


.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
.B easel alicol
selects a subset of the columns in the multiple sequence alignment(s) in the input
.I msafile,
and outputs new, smaller MSA(s) that contain only the selected columns.

.PP
Columns are selected with any of several different options:

.IP - 3
by specifying a "mask" of exactly which columns to keep
.B (\-\-mask),

.IP -
by keeping only consensus columns
.B (\-\-rfonly),

.IP -
by specifying a span of columns by start and end coord
.B (\-\-span),

.IP -
by the fraction of gaps/residues in each column
.B (\-\-gapfrac, \-\-symfrac, \-\-mingap, \-\-nogap),

.IP -
by posterior probability (alignment accuracy) annotation on each column
.B (\-\-ppcons, \-\-ppfrac, \-\-ppavg).

.PP
These options are called the "selection options".  At least one selection
option must be used. Any given selection option can only be used once.
Selection options can be combined, and the final selection mask is the
logical AND of all selections.

.PP
The
.I msafile
would typically contain just one MSA, but it may
contain 
multiple MSAs (for MSA file formats that allow that, like Stockholm)
except when the
.B \-\-mask
or
.B \-\-span
selection options are used.
The 
.B \-\-mask
selection option requires specifying a mask for a
particular MSA,
and the 
.B \-\-span
selection option requires specifying start/end coordinates for a
particular
MSA length.

.PP
The
.B \-\-rfonly
selection option requires consensus column annotation to be present.
Consensus columns can only be defined in certain MSA file formats.
In Stockholm format, 
consensus 
columns are annotated by any non-gap character in a
.B #=GC RF
annotation line.
In A2M|A3M format, consensus columns are indicated by upper case
residues.

.PP
The
.B \-\-span
selection option requires specifying start/end column numbering.
Columns in the input MSA are numbered
.IR 1..alen .
This default all-columns coordinate system can be changed to a
consensus-only coordinate system by using
the
.B \-c
option, if consensus annotation is present.
With
.BR \-c ,
the
.I C
annotated consensus columns
are numbered
.IR 1..C .
One example of when it is 
useful to work in consensus coordinates
is when they correspond to a standard reference
coordinate system (hence the "RF" name in Stockholm); the E. coli
reference coordinate system used for SSU rRNA alignment positions, for
example.

.PP
The
.B \-\-mask
option reads a mask string from an input file.
A mask string is a string of binary 1|0 characters of the same length
.I alen
(or consensus length
.I C,
if the
.B \-c
option is used).
1 means "keep", 0 means "discard" the corresponding column.

.PP
For selection options that select by posterior probability (estimated
alignment accuracy), the MSA must be in Stockholm format, and it must
have the relevant posterior probability annotation(s): #=GC PP_cons
for consensus postprobs, #=GR PP for individual residue postprobs.
Stockholm PP and PP_cons annotation is coarse-grained in 11 bins
represented by characters 0-9* (and a 12th character '.' for
nonconsensus columns), with 0 = 0.0 <= p < 0.05, 1 = 0.05 <= p <
0.15, ... , 9 = 0.85 <= p < 0.95, and * = 0.95 <= p <= 1.0.  For the
.B \-\-ppcons
and
.B \-\-ppfrac
options that enforce a minimum probability threshold,
a PP annotation character is interpreted as the minimum of its
bin's range.
For 
.BR \-\-ppavg ,
a PP annotation character is interpreted as the mean of its bin's range.
Consensus PP annotation is on consensus columns only:
each consensus column (defined by non-gap symbols in #=GC RF
annotation) has a PP_cons symbol 0-9*, and each nonconsensus (insert)
column has a PP_cons symbol of '.'. Individual PP annotation
corresponds to the residues in an individual aligned sequence.

.PP
The input MSA(s) are read in Easel's "text mode", which means that all
sequence characters are preserved verbatim, as opposed to being parsed
as amino acids or RNA|DNA nucleotides. However,
.B easel alicol
does need to know whether the input MSA has RNA secondary structure
annotation.  Removing columns can break base pair annotation, by
removing only one side of an annotated pair. Therefore 
standard Easel alphabet autodetection is performed, and the
standard Easel alphabet assertion options
.B \-\-amino | \-\-dna | \-\-rna
are present, but alphabet type is solely used to decide whether to
trigger editing of secondary structure annotation lines.  For RNA MSAs
with secondary structure annotation, when removal of a column would
remove the annotation for only one side of a base pair, the base
pairing annotation on the remaining column is converted to unpaired.

.PP
By default, the resulting new MSA(s) are written to standard output.
With the
.B \-o
option, the MSA(s) are written to a specified file instead,
and now the standard output is instead some verbose information
about the column selection process.
If the
.B \-q
(be quiet) option is added,
this verbose information is suppressed, and there is no
standard output.

.PP
If
.I msafile
is
.B \-
(a single dash) input is read from a stdin pipe.
If
.I msafile
ends in .gz it is assumed to be gzip-compressed, and is
read through a
.B gzip \-dc
pipe.
See
.B man easel
for more detail.




.SH SELECTION OPTIONS

.TP
.BI \-\-gapfrac " x"
Keep columns where the
fraction of gap characters is <
.IR x .
(Equivalently: remove columns where the
fraction is >=
.IR x .)
.I x
is > 0 and <= 1. 0 isn't allowed because
it would delete all columns. 
.B \-\-gapfrac 1
keeps only columns with at least one residue and
is equivalent to
.BR \-\-mingap .
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
and this selection is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.

.TP
.BI \-\-mask " maskfile"
Specify exactly which columns to keep, using a binary "mask"
of 1|0's. The
.I maskfile
contains a mask string of 1|0 characters of length
.I alen
(or consensus length
.IR C ,
if the
.B \-c
option is used).
1 means "keep", 0 means "discard".
Lines in
.I maskfile
that start with `#` are comments and are ignored;
whitespace in the file is ignored.

.TP
.B \-\-mingap
Only keep columns with at least one residue;
remove columns that consist entirely of gap characters.
Minimizes the overall length of the alignment without losing
sequence information.
Useful if you've extracted a subset of aligned sequences from a
larger alignment.
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-gapfrac 1" .
Consensus columns that happen to be all gaps in this MSA
will be discarded. 
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
and this selection is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.

.TP
.B \-\-nogap
Only keep columns that consist entirely of residues:
remove columns that contain any gap characters.
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-symfrac 1" .
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
and this selection is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.

.TP
.BI \-\-ppavg " x"
Calculate the mean posterior probability for all
individual aligned residues in each column; select columns
with mean >=
.IR x .
All sequences must have individual posterior probability
annotation (#=GR PP, in Stockholm).
Columns with no residues (all gaps) are considered to have
a mean posterior probability of 0 and are removed.
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
and this selection criterion is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.

.TP
.BI \-\-ppcons " x"
Keep columns with consensus posterior probability >=
.I x,
where 0 <= x <= 1.
The input
.I msafile
must be in Stockholm format, and must
have #=GC PP_cons annotation.
Nonconsensus (insert) columns have no consensus posterior probability
annotation,
so they are removed, unless
.B \-\-keepins
is also set.

.TP
.BI \-\-ppfrac " x"
Keep columns with a fraction >=
.I x
of confidently aligned residues (relative to all residues in the
column), where "confidently aligned" is
defined as a posterior probability of >=
.IR t ,
set by 
.BI \-\-ppfracthresh " t"
(t=0.95 by default).
All sequences must have individual posterior probability
annotation (#=GR PP, in Stockholm).
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
and this selection is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.

.TP
.B \-\-rfonly
Keep consensus columns, discard nonconsensus (insert) columns.
MSA must have consensus column annotation, so it must be in
Stockholm format with a #=GC RF annotation line, or in A2M|A3M format.

.TP
.BI \-\-span " pos1:pos2"
Keep columns from position
.I pos1
to position
.I pos2,
numbering all the columns 1..alen by default.
With the
.B \-c
option,
these position coordinates are in consensus column coordinates 1..C
instead; keep all columns (including nonconsensus inserts) between consensus columns
.I pos1
and
.IR pos2 ,
inclusive.
.I pos1
and
.I pos2
can be separated by any non-numeric and non-whitespace character:
"42:100", "42-100", and "42..100" all work.
Coordinates must be 1 <= pos1 <= pos2 <= alen.
As a special case, leaving out
.I pos2
is interpreted as requesting a suffix: for example, 
"42:" means from column 42 to the end of the MSA.

.TP
.BI \-\-symfrac " x"
Keep columns where the fraction of residue characters is >=
.IR x.
(Equivalently, remove columns where the fraction is <
.IR x .)
.I x
is > 0 and <= 1. 0 isn't allowed because it would do nothing; all columns
would be kept, even all-gap ones. 
.B \-\-symfrac 1
keeps only columns that are all residues, with no gaps at all, and
is equivalent to
.BR \-\-nogap .
With the
.B \-\-keeprf
option, consensus columns are always kept,
meaning that the selection action is only applied to nonconsensus (insert) columns.



.SH OTHER OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-c
Instead of 1..alen, column coordinates are with respect to the annotated consensus
columns 1..C. Nonconsensus (insert)
columns are unnumbered. The MSA must have consensus column annotation.
This affects the start/end coordinates used by
.B \-\-span
and the length of the mask string expected by
.BR \-\-mask .
It does not affect the length of the mask string written
by
.BR \-\-outmask ,
which is always the full mask for all columns (length alen);
use
.BR \-\-outrfmask
to save the final mask in 1..C consensus coordinates.

.TP
.B \-w
Calculate weighted frequencies or averages for columns, using sequence
weights annotated in the MSA. (The default is to calculate unweighted
frequencies/averages.)  The input
.I msafile
must be in a format that allows weight annotation; currently this is
only Stockholm format. If weights are not present, they are
treated as 1.0.
Requires using a relevant selection option:
.BR \-\-gapfrac ,
.BR \-\-ppfrac ,
.BR \-\-ppavg ,
.BR \-\-symfrac.
For calculating MSA weights, see
.BR "easel weight" .

.TP
.B \-\-keepins
When applying a
.B \-\-ppcons 
selection, keep all nonconsensus (insert) columns and only apply the
selection rule to consensus columns.
Otherwise, by default
.B  \-\-ppcons
would discard nonconsensus columns because they have no consensus posterior
probability.
This can be useful if all you're trying to do is remove uncertainly
aligned consensus columns, and maybe you're going to apply some other selection rule to
insertions. (Requires
.BR \-\-ppcons .)

.TP
.B \-\-keeprf
With thresholded selection options that could delete a consensus column and thus alter and break a
reference coordinate system that we want to preserve (
.BR \-\-gapfrac ,
.BR \-\-mingap ,
.BR \-\-nogap ,
.BR \-\-symfrac ,
.BR \-\-ppfrac ,
.BR \-\-ppavg ),
keep all consensus columns even if they don't satisfy the selection
criterion. That is, only apply these selection criterion to nonconsensus
(insert) positions.
Note that
.B \-\-ppcons
is not on this list; since
.B \-\-ppcons
only affects consensus columns in the first place,
it doesn't make sense to combine it with
.B \-\-keeprf.


.TP
.BI \-\-outmask " outfile"
Write the final mask (the logical AND of all the selection
options that were used) to 
.I outfile
as a mask string of length alen.
The
.I msafile
may only have one MSA in it, because mask files
can only contain one mask.


.TP
.BI \-\-outrfmask " outfile"
Write the final mask (the logical AND of all the selection
options that were used), for consensus columns only, to 
.I outfile
as a mask string of length C. The MSA must have consensus
column annotation.
The
.I msafile
may only have one MSA in it, because mask files
can only contain one mask.

.TP
.BI \-\-ppfracthresh " t"
With (and requiring) the
.B \-\-ppfrac
option, sets the threshold posterior probability
for the 
.BI \-\-ppfrac " x"
selection, which selects columns that
have a fraction >=
.I x
of individual postprob annotations of >=
.I t.
The default is 0.95, which means
that
.BI \-\-ppfrac " x"
selects columns where a fraction of at least
.I x
of the residues are annotated with a '*' (the highest
bin of alignment accuracy in PP annotations).

.TP
.B \-\-small
Small memory mode.
The input 
.I msafile
and the output 
must both be in Pfam format (non-interleaved, 1 line/sequence Stockholm
format),
and the alphabet for the MSA file must be asserted with one
of the
.BR \-\-amino |
.BR \-\-dna   |
.B  \-\-rna
options.
The
.B \-w
option can't be used; 
frequency-based selections use only unweighted frequencies,
because the legacy small-memory parser does not parse sequence weights.
The
.I msafile
will be processed without ever reading a complete MSA into memory,
which can be useful for some extremely large MSAs (such as SSU/LSU
rRNA alignments). Because small memory mode requires two
read passes over the file, the input
.I msafile
must actually be a file; it cannot be `-` (a stdin stream).


.SH STANDARD EASEL OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-h
Help: print a brief reminder of command line usage and a summary of
all available options, and exit.

.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the new smaller MSA(s) to
.IR outfile ,
rather than to standard output.
Some verbose diagnostic information about the selection process
is written to stdout instead (unless
.B \-q
is also used).

.TP
.B \-q
With (and requiring) the
.B \-o
option, silences the verbose diagnostic output;
the MSA is written to the file requested by
.BR \-o ,
and no output is written to stdout.

.TP
.B \-\-amino
Assert that the 
.I msafile 
contains protein sequences.

.TP
.B \-\-dna
Assert that the 
.I msafile 
contains DNA sequences. 

.TP
.BI \-\-informat " fmt"
Assert that input
.I msafile
is in alignment format
.IR fmt ,
bypassing format autodetection.
Choices for 
.I fmt
are
.BR a2m|a3m|afa|clustal|clustallike|pfam|phylip|phylips|psiblast|selex|stockholm .
.I fmt
is case-insensitive (e.g.
.B afa
or
.B AFA
both work).


.TP
.BI \-\-outformat " fmt"
Write the new smaller alignment in alignment format
.IR fmt .
Choices for
.I fmt
are
.BR a2m|a3m|afa|clustal|clustallike|pfam|phylip|phylips|psiblast|selex|stockholm .
.I fmt
is case-insensitive (e.g.
.B afa
or
.BR AFA
both work). Default is to use the same format as the input MSA file.
If the input
.I msafile
contains multiple MSAs,
the output format must allow multiple MSAs: this typically means
using Stockholm,
though phylip and phylips format also allow multiple MSAs per file.
Incompatible with
.BR \-\-small ,
because
.BR \-\-small
requires the input and output MSA file to both be in one-block
Stockholm ("Pfam") format.


.TP 
.B \-\-rna
Assert that the 
.I msafile 
contains RNA sequences. 




.SH SEE ALSO

.B man easel
for more information about 
Easel miniapps, their shared behaviors, and 
some standardized options.

